## Django Development Standards

### Model Development Rules
- Keep all models in `models.py` with proper relationships
- Use Django's built-in User model extension (CustomUser)
- Always include `created_at` and `updated_at` timestamp fields
- Use proper field types (EmailField, URLField, FileField, etc.)
- Add descriptive `__str__` methods for better admin representation
- Implement proper Meta classes with unique_together constraints

```
# Standard Model Pattern
class Student(models.Model):
    user = models.OneToOneField(CustomUser, on_delete=models.CASCADE)
    roll_number = models.CharField(max_length=20, unique=True)
    course = models.ForeignKey(Course, on_delete=models.CASCADE)
    session = models.ForeignKey(Session, on_delete=models.CASCADE)
    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)
    updated_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now=True)
    
    class Meta:
        unique_together = ['roll_number', 'session']
    
    def __str__(self):
        return f"{self.roll_number} - {self.user.first_name} {self.user.last_name}"
```

### View Development Rules
- Always use `@login_required` decorator for protected views
- Implement proper role-based permission checking
- Use try-except blocks for error handling
- Add Django messages for user feedback
- Use class-based views when appropriate (ListView, CreateView, etc.)
